494114, 'end': 3494316, 'confidence': 0.99999, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'often', 'start': 3494338, 'end': 3494572, 'confidence': 0.98651, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'used', 'start': 3494626, 'end': 3494940, 'confidence': 0.51097, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'in', 'start': 3495010, 'end': 3495388, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'quantum', 'start': 3495474, 'end': 3495848, 'confidence': 0.99839, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'mechanics,', 'start': 3495864, 'end': 3496780, 'confidence': 0.99996, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'are', 'start': 3498000, 'end': 3498460, 'confidence': 0.98878, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'being', 'start': 3498530, 'end': 3498764, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'able', 'start': 3498802, 'end': 3499100, 'confidence': 0.62332, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'to', 'start': 3499170, 'end': 3499548, 'confidence': 0.89, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'come', 'start': 3499634, 'end': 3499884, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'together', 'start': 3499922, 'end': 3500460, 'confidence': 0.51204, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'with', 'start': 3500610, 'end': 3501068, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'active', 'start': 3501154, 'end': 3501576, 'confidence': 0.99943, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'inference', 'start': 3501608, 'end': 3501976, 'confidence': 0.83094, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'this', 'start': 3502008, 'end': 3502204, 'confidence': 0.99999, 'speaker': 'B'}, {'text': 'way.', 'start': 3502242, 'end': 3502830, 'confidence': 0.99989, 'speaker': 'B'}]}, {'confidence': 0.9440793644067806, 'end': 3673540, 'speaker': 'A', 'start': 3503520, 'text': "Yeah, something that comes to mind when you mention this, I think there's also like a recent avenue of research in this area where people and it's again kind of generated by the fact that you don't know the graph beforehand many times. And I think kind of the old school approach was, well, you construct it based on some rules, right? Like you're going to say, I don't know, some things are similar, I'm going to put an edge between them and you define similar in whatever way you like and so on. And there was this kind of recent trend where what you try to do is kind of latent graph inference, or some people call it manifold learning, if you think of the graph as some sort of manifold. But this kind of very informally speaking and essentially what you would do is like you would map whatever you try to learn the raw observations into some latent space. And that's where you actually construct the graph. You construct the graph in the latent space rather than kind of in the raw space. So that might be kind of a way to deal with fuzziness as well. Right? Because then I guess you might lose some of these kind of very concrete one to one mappings because you might learn some node in the latent space that maybe corresponds to three or four concepts kind of mixed together. There's all these kind of nice experiments with neurons in deep networks, kind of visualized, and they learn maybe kind of a mixture of concepts. If you see what actually activates that neuron is actually maybe a few classes or different kinds of things. It's not necessarily a single thing. So it could be something very similar here where you have some very entangled representations that are kind of distilled in this latent graph. And then in a way, at least in concept space, even if in the latent space, that's still kind of a very clear combinatorial structure with respect to kind of your raw observations. That structure can still kind of encode the fuzziness of the world to some degree because you have this kind of mixture of concepts that got distilled in the same node or things like that. Or maybe some concepts could be represented by multiple nodes depending on what way you see these concepts. There might be all sorts of variations or concept or points of view and so on. So I think kind of latent graph inference could be quite an interesting way maybe to address some of these issues we were discussing. Although I think it kind of died off a bit in the recent year, at least as far as I've seen. There were a few slightly fewer papers on the topic.", 'words': [{'text': 'Yeah,', 'start': 3503520, 'end': 3504270, 'confidence': 0.99983, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'something', 'start': 3505360, 'end': 3505820, 'confidence': 0.98343, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'that', 'start': 3505890, 'end': 3506316, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'comes', 'start': 3506418, 'end': 3506684, 'confidence': 0.99999, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'to', 'start': 3506722, 'end': 3506876, 'confidence': 0.8, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'mind', 'start': 3506898, 'end': 3507228, 'confidence': 0.99997, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'when', 'start': 3507314, 'end': 3507516, 'confidence': 0.54681, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'you', 'start': 3507538, 'end': 3507676, 'confidence': 0.99999, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'mention', 'start': 3507698, 'end': 3507896, 'confidence': 0.21055, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'this,', 'start': 3507938, 'end': 3508144, 'confidence': 0.99774, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'I', 'start': 3508182, 'end': 3508288, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'think', 'start': 3508294, 'end': 3508656, 'confidence': 0.97911, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': "there's", 'start': 3508758, 'end': 3509036, 'confidence': 0.87516, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'also', 'start': 3509068, 'end': 3509312, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'like', 'start': 3509366, 'end': 3509584, 'confidence': 0.99514, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'a', 'start': 3509622, 'end': 3510210, 'confidence': 1.0, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'recent', 'start': 3510980, 'end': 3511900, 'confidence': 0.99998, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'avenue', 'start': 3512060, 'end': 3512556, 'confidence': 0.99954, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'of', 'start': 3512588, 'end': 3512688, 'confidence': 0.99, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'research', 'start': 3512694, 'end': 3512960, 'confidence': 0.99996, 'speaker': 'A'}, {'text': 'i